## 2. Overall Description

### 2.1 Product Perspective
Cine Verse is a standalone, client-side web application. It is not part of a larger system and has no dependency on internal infrastructure — its sole external dependency is the TMDB API for movie data.

### 2.2 Product Functions (High-Level)
- Accept a search query and retrieve matching movies from TMDB.
- Display a list of search results with poster thumbnails.
- Allow the user to select a result and view full movie details.
- Animate transitions between UI states (idle, loading, results, error).
- Handle invalid input, empty results, and API failures gracefully.

### 2.3 User Classes and Characteristics
| User Class | Description |
|---|---|
| General User | Casual visitor searching for movie information; requires no technical knowledge. |
| Evaluator/Instructor | Reviews the project for functional correctness and design quality. |

### 2.4 Operating Environment
- Runs in modern web browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari).
- No installation required for end users; accessible via URL or local development server.
- Compatible with desktop and mobile viewport sizes.

### 2.5 Design and Implementation Constraints
- Dependent on TMDB API availability, rate limits, and data accuracy.
- Requires an active internet connection at all times (no offline mode).
- Requires a valid TMDB API key, stored via environment variables and not committed to source control.

### 2.6 Assumptions and Dependencies
- TMDB API remains free/accessible under its current terms for the duration of development.
- The chosen animation library (if any) remains compatible with the frontend framework used.

